  "knowledge": "The framework rolls the transaction back, and PostgreSQL confirms the ROLLBACK, so the client knows that nothing committed.",
  "api_outcome": {
    "reported": "rolled_back",
    "basis": "atomic() exited with the exception raised inside it, so Atomic.__exit__ rolled back",
    "contract": "Django documents transaction.atomic() as: \"If the block of code is successfully completed, the changes are committed to the database. If there is an exception, the changes are rolled back.\" Atomic.__exit__ commits only when exc_type is None and needs_rollback is false; otherwise it rolls back (django/db/transaction.py). A savepoint rollback that raises django.db.Error sets needs_rollback and does not replace the original exception. A root rollback that raises django.db.Error is swallowed and connection.close() is called. An exception that is not django.db.Error is not caught, so needs_rollback stays false. The DatabaseError admonition says: \"After such an error, the transaction is broken and Django will perform a rollback at the end of the atomic block.\" Model.save uses mark_for_rollback_on_error, which sets needs_rollback when a database error is raised inside an atomic block.",
    "error": {
      "class": "AppError",
      "code": null,
      "message": "application error: the request fails"
    },
    "steps": {}
  },
